    abstract: "Nafs Mama investigates how the “nafs” behind family recipes can be understood, documented, and passed down through generations. A written recipe can preserve ingredients, measurements, and instructions, but the nafs of a dish goes beyond following these steps. It is reflected in the cook’s way of handling ingredients, judging textures, adjusting flavours, timing each stage, and making decisions through experience. The project began with my mother’s cooking video and recipe sheets. When I attempted to recreate her dish using my documentation, I was unable to achieve the same taste, which led me to question how the nafs behind her cooking could be transferred to someone else. Through exploratory surveys, research into food memories, tacit knowledge and psychology, and practical experiments, the project investigates how the nafs can be captured beyond a written recipe. It explores ways of documenting not only how a dish is prepared, but also the individual knowledge, sensory understanding, and intuition that give it its familiar character. Ultimately, the research aims to explore how someone can recreate not just a family recipe, but the nafs that makes it their family’s own.",
